{"id":90,"course_id":26,"title":"Postmortems and Corrective Actions","summary":"Turn painful failures into system improvements without blame.","sequence":3,"body_simplified":"## Mental model\nA postmortem should explain impact, contributing factors, detection gaps, response behavior, mitigation, root causes, and corrective actions. The best actions reduce recurrence or impact; they are owned, dated, and reviewable.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice converting a vague action like 'be more careful' into specific work: add canary gate, tighten alert, fix dashboard, change runbook, add test, reduce timeout, improve rollback.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nWrite a postmortem summary that includes what went well, what made response harder, action owners, and how the platform will verify improvement.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","body_traditional":"## Mental model\nA postmortem should explain impact, contributing factors, detection gaps, response behavior, mitigation, root causes, and corrective actions. The best actions reduce recurrence or impact; they are owned, dated, and reviewable.\n\n## What to practice\nPractice converting a vague action like 'be more careful' into specific work: add canary gate, tighten alert, fix dashboard, change runbook, add test, reduce timeout, improve rollback.\n\n## Practice checkpoint\nWrite a postmortem summary that includes what went well, what made response harder, action owners, and how the platform will verify improvement.\n\n## Operator habit\nWrite down the symptom, the evidence, the suspected owner, and the safest next command before changing any live object. This turns scattered platform knowledge into a repeatable engineering workflow.","pinyin":"Scenario: You are the platform engineer on call for a realistic team environment.\nChecklist:\n- State the user impact in one sentence.\n- Identify the system boundary and owner.\n- Collect read-only evidence first.\n- Propose the smallest reversible change.\n- Capture what should become a dashboard, alert, runbook, or automation.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\n$ kubectl describe pod POD_NAME -n NAMESPACE\n$ kubectl logs deploy/APP -n NAMESPACE --since=15m","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-incident-response-reliability","course_category":"Incident Response","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":421,"simplified":"Incident Response and Reliability Leadership","traditional":"Incident Response and Reliability Leadership","pinyin":"Incident Response","definition":"Practice incident command, severity, mitigation, communications, timelines, postmortems, corrective actions, and game days for platform roles."},{"id":422,"simplified":"Evidence","traditional":"Evidence","pinyin":"Debugging","definition":"Observed facts from commands, dashboards, logs, traces, events, or cloud APIs."},{"id":423,"simplified":"Blast radius","traditional":"Blast radius","pinyin":"Reliability","definition":"The set of users, tenants, workloads, or systems affected by a failure or change."},{"id":424,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A planned path to return to a known-good state when a change causes harm."},{"id":425,"simplified":"Runbook","traditional":"Runbook","pinyin":"Operations","definition":"A step-by-step guide that helps responders diagnose and mitigate an incident safely."}],"flashcards":[{"id":253,"lesson_id":90,"prompt":"What is the main goal of Postmortems and Corrective Actions?","answer":"Turn painful failures into system improvements without blame.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":254,"lesson_id":90,"prompt":"Why collect evidence before changing live systems?","answer":"It reduces guessing, protects users, and makes the fix reviewable.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":255,"lesson_id":90,"prompt":"What should a learner produce after each lab?","answer":"A clear diagnosis, a safe action plan, and a reusable note for future incidents.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
